<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Voice-capable release manifest (Build 34 Android Voice Scan;
      generalized by ANDROID-VOICE-01).

      This file is NOT part of any ordinary build. android/app/build.gradle
      substitutes it for src/release/AndroidManifest.xml when EITHER governed
      selector resolves exactly "true":

        - `kscan.voiceCertification` (Gradle property) / KSCAN_VOICE_CERTIFICATION
          (environment) -- set in exactly one committed place, the
          `staging-certification` EAS build profile in eas.json. This
          selector's meaning is specifically "this is the staging-certification
          build" and is never reinterpreted to mean anything else.
        - `kscan.voiceNativeCapability` (Gradle property) / KSCAN_VOICE_NATIVE_CAPABILITY
          (environment) -- a non-public, generic "materialize native Voice
          capability" selector that eas.json commits to NO profile (production
          included). It is supplied out of band by a release operator only at
          an explicitly authorized production Voice activation; see
          docs/build34-android-voice-production-readiness-runbook.md.

      Both selectors choose this SAME manifest because its content (grant
      RECORD_AUDIO, forbid everything else) is identical either way -- only
      android/app/build.gradle's guards differ in which EAS profile may set
      which selector (staging-certification for the first, staging-certification
      or production for the second). Every other build -- ordinary production,
      staging, preview, development, and any local `gradlew bundleRelease` --
      leaves both selectors unset and therefore uses
      src/release/AndroidManifest.xml, which grants no microphone permission
      at all.

      WHY A PROFILE-SPECIFIC MANIFEST INSTEAD OF app.json:
      Android is NATIVE_AUTHORITATIVE here (config/native-config-authority.json),
      so android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml is what actually ships.
      Adding RECORD_AUDIO to app.json + the main manifest would broaden the
      PRODUCTION artifact by default, before any Voice activation is
      authorized. Voice Scan needs the permission only in a build that
      explicitly requested it, so the grant lives only in this manifest
      variant, and app.json continues to declare RECORD_AUDIO blocked --
      which remains true of the default/production artifact.

      WHAT THIS FILE MUST ALWAYS BE: a strict superset of
      src/release/AndroidManifest.xml plus the Voice permission block below.
      scripts/check-native-config-parity.js (run via
      __tests__/nativeConfigParityGate.test.js) enforces that relationship,
      for both declared exceptions in
      config/native-config-authority.json, so the manifests cannot drift.

      WHAT THIS FILE MUST NEVER CONTAIN: a foreground-service microphone
      permission, a <service> element of any kind, a background-capture
      permission, or any change to package/applicationId/signing. The
      microphone is requested just-in-time by the user's explicit tap
      (hooks/useVoiceScan.ts -> KScanVoiceNativeModule.requestPermissions)
      and released when the app leaves the foreground (ProcessLifecycleOwner
      in KScanVoiceNativeModule.kt).
    -->

    <!-- Carried verbatim from src/release/AndroidManifest.xml. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW" tools:node="remove"/>

    <!--
      Voice Scan certification grant. tools:node="replace" supersedes the
      tools:node="remove" that src/main/AndroidManifest.xml applies to this
      same permission; a build-type source set has higher manifest-merger
      priority than main, so the merged certification manifest grants
      RECORD_AUDIO while the default release manifest still removes it.
      This is an artifact-verified claim: confirm it against the merged
      manifest of the built AAB before certification (see
      docs/watchlist-tier2-operations.md, "Voice Scan certification
      permission proof").
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" tools:node="replace"/>

    <!--
      CAPTURE permissions, explicitly denied even in the certification
      profile. Voice Scan is strictly foreground and strictly just-in-time;
      there is no capture path that could need either of these, and naming
      them here means a library that later contributes one cannot slip it
      into the certification artifact unnoticed. Both are also declared in
      config/native-config-authority.json's mustRemainRemovedEverywhere for
      each Voice exception, so this file and the governed contract agree.

      MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS USED TO BE LISTED HERE AND IS NOT ANY MORE
      (Android Repair 03). It was grouped with the two capture permissions
      above, but it is not a capture permission: expo-audio contributes it
      for PLAYBACK ROUTING. setAudioModeAsync -- which
      services/avatars/stylistAudioPlayback.ts calls as the first statement
      of every Elise/stylist speech playback -- reaches
      AudioManager.setMode()/setSpeakerphoneOn() unconditionally
      (expo-audio's AudioModule.updatePlaySoundThroughEarpiece(), invoked
      regardless of shouldRouteThroughEarpiece's value), and both of those
      APIs require MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS.

      This manifest is selected for the staging-certification build AND for
      a future production build carrying KSCAN_VOICE_NATIVE_CAPABILITY
      (ANDROID-VOICE-01), and Elise speech is reachable in both: the
      resolved staging-certification env sets
      EXPO_PUBLIC_AI_STYLIST_ENABLED=true, and the stylist voice preference
      defaults to ON for a device with no recorded choice
      (stores/stylistVoicePreferenceStore.ts). Removing the permission here
      therefore stripped audio routing from the exact artifact that still
      plays Elise speech. It is not re-granted by this file -- expo-audio's
      own manifest contributes it through the ordinary merge, exactly as it
      does for production (see android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml).

      Removing it here never restricted the microphone: RECORD_AUDIO,
      FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE and CAPTURE_AUDIO_OUTPUT are the
      capture controls, and all three are unchanged by Repair 03.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE" tools:node="remove"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAPTURE_AUDIO_OUTPUT" tools:node="remove"/>

</manifest>
